# CHANGELOG.md is append-only by construction, so a union merge is the correct
# resolution for it.
#
# scripts/add_changelog_entry.py never rewrites the "## [Unreleased]" block; it
# inserts one entry at the end of its section and leaves every other byte
# alone. Two branches adding to the SAME section therefore collide at one
# position, and "keep both entries" is always right. Before this file existed
# that resolution was done by hand: merging seven pull requests on 2026-10-20
# took thirteen of them, all identical, because each merge put the remaining
# branches behind main again.
#
# Measured, not assumed, by reproducing both shapes in a throwaway repository:
#
#   two branches appending at the same anchor
#     without union -> conflict, markers in the file
#     with union    -> clean, both entries present, one [Unreleased] heading,
#                      the new entry under [Unreleased] where it belongs
#
#   a release pull request (renaming [Unreleased] to a version) against a
#   branch appending to [Unreleased]
#     without union -> merges clean, the entry lands under the RELEASED
#                      version, and check_changelog_structure.py passes
#     with union    -> identical
#
# That second shape is worth being clear about: it is NOT caused by this file.
# The rename and the append do not overlap textually, so git has no reason to
# stop, union driver or not. The hazard predates this change and is unchanged
# by it - see the note in RELEASE_PROCESS.md.
#
# What union cannot do is notice that it was wrong. It never reports a
# conflict, so this file is only safe because the thing it resolves is
# genuinely always "keep both": that property comes from the insert-only
# writer, and if entries ever start being edited in place rather than appended,
# this line has to go.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
